<!--
  THE PROCEDURES

  What the lab will do to a body, what it wants brought to it, and where on you it goes.

  A procedure is a CATALOGUE RECORD in the same idiom as a building: mergeable by key, extended by
  anybody who ships a file with a <kingdomprocedures> root, and overridden by key re-use in load
  order. A hardcoded catalog is a defect (STANDARDS 6).

  THE FIRST RULE, AND EVERYTHING ELSE FOLLOWS FROM IT: `Grants` names a PART CLASS - never a
  creature, and never "a creature's power". That is what makes this registry a contract rather than
  a list. A modded creature carrying `PoisonOnHit` is a lawful source for the envenomed sting the
  day that mod ships, with no entry of ours and no code of ours. It is also why there is no creature
  named anywhere in this file, including in the two records that exist BECAUSE two creatures carry
  the same class at wildly different numbers (see Magnitude, below).

  Every attribute is optional but Key, Grants and Slots:

    Key         Registry identity. A later file re-using a Key owns that record whole.
    DisplayName How the slate reads it. Falls back to the Key.
    Class       I riders / II defences and utility / III limbs / IV named. The class decides which
                rung of hall does the work and nothing else; MinRung may disagree deliberately.
    Grants      The IPart class. Never a creature.
    Slots       BodyPart.Type names, comma separated - exactly CyberneticsBaseItem.Slots' own shape
                (D/XRL/World/Parts/CyberneticsBaseItem.cs:14,155-157). Checked against the PLAYER's
                own anatomy, never against a table: a founder with no tail and no free arm is
                refused BY NAME, and that refusal is the whole rationing mechanism.
    SlotCategories  BodyPartCategory names, comma separated (23 of them,
                D/XRL/World/Anatomy/BodyPartCategory.cs:8-52). Omitted admits any live category.
                This is how a True Kin, a robot player and a slime player each get a different legal
                set of procedures FOR FREE, derived, with no genotype list anywhere in the code.
    Source      part | limb | mutation. Three source kinds, three write paths.
    Attach      body | weapon. READ THE NEXT PARAGRAPH.
    MinRung     0 slab / 1 vat-house / 2 grafting hall / 3 chimeric theatre.
    Cost        Drams, from the city's dedicated stores.
    Bits        Bits, in vanilla's own bit-string vocabulary.
    StaffDays   Days of the hall's REAL labour. Never a timer: a hall with no hands works no days
                at all (Addendum 8 clause 2).
    Preserved   Kept parts consumed. One creature, one limb.
    Creeds      Standing this costs, in the -Faction removal idiom the QoL vocabulary already
                speaks, spent through the shipped AdjustStanding path.
    Knowledge   Roster tokens the city must hold, in KingdomZoningRules.Knows' own grammar.
    Magnitude   Field:Low-High. See below.

  ATTACH, AND WHY IT EXISTS. One melee hit fires "AttackerHit" on the ATTACKER
  (D/XRL/World/Parts/Combat.cs:1146-1154 - Attacker.FireEvent at :1154) and "WeaponHit" on the
  WEAPON (:1178-1186 - Weapon.FireEvent at :1186). A part whose Register asks only for the weapon
  event is INERT if it is copied onto a founder's torso; nothing there will ever fire it. The
  weapon a natural attack carries is the limb's own DefaultBehavior object
  (BodyPart.GetFirstValidWeapon, D/.../BodyPart.cs:2874-2895; Combat.cs:729-756 hands it through as
  the Weapon argument; Combat.cs:1636-1639 returns early on a null weapon, so there is no unarmed
  branch at all). Every record below carries the bit, and every one of them was decided by reading
  that class's own Register method, cited in the record. `Attach="weapon"` is refused at a slot that
  bears no natural weapon, by name: "there is nothing on you there for it to ride."

  MAGNITUDE, AND WHY IT EXISTS. Under "your sting is its sting" the granted numbers are the source
  instance's own (IPart.DeepCopy, D/XRL/World/IPart.cs:401-435, copies the public fields verbatim).
  ReflectDamage ships at 5 on one creature and at 100 on another - and those are not the same
  product at the same price. So the class ships as TWO records, told apart by a band on the source's
  own field. Nothing is clamped: the founder still gets exactly what they brought home. The band
  only decides which slate the thing they brought home appears on, and what it costs. It names a
  FIELD, so the first rule survives intact.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND IS NOT COMING. Every self-replication and duplication class, Invisibility,
  WallWalker, Metamorphosis, OldElectricalGeneration, and the whole [Spicy] block. Not a convention:
  KingdomProcedureRules.Blocklist refuses them at LOAD, so a third party's file naming one fails
  loudly on the day it ships rather than quietly on the day somebody clicks. Boundary powers arrive
  as named procedures, one ruling each, or they do not arrive (Addendum 22 D1).
-->
<kingdomprocedures Schema="1">

  <!-- ==================================================================== CLASS I ==
    Riders. The hall's ordinary work: your attacks do a second thing. Every one of them is
    saveable, vanilla-tuned, and worth exactly what the creature you killed was worth, because
    what you get is a copy of that creature's own part with that creature's own numbers in it.
  -->

  <!--
    D/XRL/World/Parts/StickOnHit.cs:18-23 registers "AttackerHit" AND "WeaponHit", unconditionally.
    Body-attach is proven, not assumed. Applies Stuck with the source's own save target (:26-45),
    and the source is a soup sludge's liquid-themed pseudopod - so which sludge you brought home
    decides how hard the grip is to break, and the record never has to know that.
  -->
  <procedure Key="tarrygrip" DisplayName="the tarry grip"
             Class="I" Grants="StickOnHit"
             Slots="Arm,Hand,Tail,Pseudopod"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Protoplasmic,Mollusk,Jelly"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="20" Bits="002" StaffDays="6"
             Preserved="1"
             Creeds="-Templar">
    <discloses Text="what you strike sticks where it stands, unless it shrugs the grip off" />
    <discloses Text="how hard it is to shrug off is whatever it was on the thing you took it from" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    D/XRL/World/Parts/SapOnPenetration.cs:45-50 registers "AttackerAfterDamage" and "AttackerHit"
    and NO weapon event at all - the cleanest body-attach on this list. The drain is PERMANENT
    (GetStat(Stat).BaseValue -= num, :88-90) and natural-only (:66-67), which is what keeps it
    synergising with Class III limbs rather than with every carbide hammer in the world.

    WHICH STAT IT DRAINS IS THE CREATURE YOU BROUGHT HOME. The sap family bakes the stat in per
    blueprint, DeepCopy carries it, and this record never learns the word "Willpower".
  -->
  <procedure Key="sapskiss" DisplayName="the sap's kiss"
             Class="I" Grants="SapOnPenetration"
             Slots="Face,Hand,Arm"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="25" Bits="002" StaffDays="6"
             Preserved="1"
             Creeds="-Templar">
    <discloses Text="a hit that gets through takes something from what it hit, and does not give it back" />
    <discloses Text="which thing it takes is whatever the sap you brought home was taking" />
    <discloses Text="it works from your own body and not from a weapon you are holding" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    D/XRL/World/Parts/SapChargeOnHit.cs:165-170 registers "AttackerHit" and "WeaponHit" - body-clean.
    ForceCharge arcs the stolen charge into your own storage if you have any and into the air if you
    do not, so there is no robot assumption anywhere in it. Against flesh it does nothing, which is
    honest rationing rather than a nerf.
  -->
  <procedure Key="galvanicleech" DisplayName="the galvanic leech"
             Class="I" Grants="SapChargeOnHit"
             Slots="Hand,Face,Arm"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="25" Bits="0023" StaffDays="6"
             Preserved="1"
             Creeds="-Mechanimists">
    <discloses Text="what you strike loses its charge to you, if it had any" />
    <discloses Text="against anything that was never wound up it does nothing at all" />
    <discloses Text="the Mechanimists will hear that you had it done" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    WEAPON-ATTACH, AND THE RECORD SAYS SO. D/XRL/World/Parts/LifeDrainOnHit.cs:32-36 registers
    "WeaponHit" and nothing else - grafted onto a torso it could never fire once. It goes onto the
    natural weapon standing at the slot, and the hall refuses a slot that has no claw on it.

    The strongest rider on the board at its shipped numbers, which is why it is the only Class I
    here priced above the hall. RealityDistortionBased, so normality shuts it off: honest
    counterplay the founder can be walked into.
  -->
  <procedure Key="leechpseudopod" DisplayName="the leech pseudopod"
             Class="I" Grants="LifeDrainOnHit"
             Slots="Arm,Hand,Pseudopod,Tentacle"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Protoplasmic,Mollusk,Jelly"
             Source="part" Attach="weapon"
             MinRung="3" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="45" Bits="0023" StaffDays="10"
             Preserved="2"
             Creeds="-Templar,-Mechanimists">
    <discloses Text="what your own claws open bleeds toward you, and you are the better for it" />
    <discloses Text="it rides the limb's own weapon, so it wants a limb that already bites" />
    <discloses Text="under normality it stops entirely, and anything carrying normality knows that" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    WEAPON-ATTACH. D/XRL/World/Parts/DrunkOnHit.cs:10-14 registers "WeaponHit" only.
    Force-feeds the defender four doses of wine through the liquid system - pure vanilla machinery,
    a joke that is also a real debuff, and the most Qud sentence in this file.

    Known vanilla oddity, ours to know and not ours to fix: the part fetches
    GetSpecialEffectChanceEvent and discards the result, then rolls raw (:27-29), so effect-chance
    modifiers do not reach it. Not a defect of this record; it will confuse anyone reading logs.
  -->
  <procedure Key="vintnersfang" DisplayName="the vintner's fang"
             Class="I" Grants="DrunkOnHit"
			 Slots="Hand,Arm"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod"
             Source="part" Attach="weapon"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="20" Bits="002" StaffDays="5"
             Preserved="1"
             Creeds="-Templar">
    <discloses Text="what your own claws open gets a mouthful of wine it did not ask for" />
    <discloses Text="it rides the limb's own weapon, so it wants a limb that already bites" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    The same audited weapon rider, but seated on a muzzle rather than a hand. This is the
    body-shaped half of Addendum 17: a city learns it only while a living snapjaw body can teach
    its vat-workers how a face-borne natural weapon is joined. Culture opens trail-reading;
    species opens anatomy, and the two facts never stand in for one another.
  -->
  <procedure Key="moonfang" DisplayName="the moon-vintner's fang"
             Class="I" Grants="DrunkOnHit"
             Slots="Face"
             SlotCategories="Animal"
             Source="part" Attach="weapon"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:muzzlecraft"
             Cost="20" Bits="002" StaffDays="5"
             Preserved="1"
             Creeds="-Templar">
    <discloses Text="the city learned the joining from a living snapjaw muzzle, not from a tale about one" />
    <discloses Text="what your own bite opens gets a mouthful of wine it did not ask for" />
    <discloses Text="it wants a face that already bears a natural weapon" />
  </procedure>

  <!-- =================================================================== CLASS II ==
    Defences and utility. The same ordinary work, pointed inward.
  -->

  <!--
    THE AUTHOR'S OWN CANDIDATE, and it is admitted with its teeth showing.

    SporePuffer is a mutation class (D/XRL/World/Parts/Mutation/SporePuffer.cs:10, ": BaseMutation")
    and BaseMutation extends IPart, so both verbs could carry it. Source="part" is DELIBERATE:
    DeepCopy carries PuffObject and ColorString for free (both public fields, IPart.cs:410-426),
    while the mutation verb re-instantiates by NAME and would puff generic spores - the exact defect
    the precedent had to hand-patch. The part verb makes that class of bug structurally impossible.

    Answers on the bearer (BeginTakeActionEvent, :33-40), so body-attach. The bearer is safe from
    their own gas (Gas.Creator = ParentObject, :84). Hostiles weight adjacent cells at 97 (:96-103),
    a free melee deterrent. And the hidden rider: it registers "ApplySpores" and returns false
    (:107, :114-117), so a grafted gland makes you unpuffable - stated on the slate, because a free
    thing nobody is told about is a thing nobody has.

    THE PRICE IS DISCLOSED IN WORDS. The trigger is "adjacent and not allied" (:69) - a merely
    NEUTRAL resident of your own city trips it, and once the gas exists it infects anyone but its
    creator. Walking your own market can seed fungal infections in your own people. It ships anyway,
    because it is honest and it is Qud and the friction doctrine wants exactly this; but it says so
    before you commit, not afterwards.
  -->
  <procedure Key="sporegills" DisplayName="the brooding gills"
             Class="II" Grants="SporePuffer"
             Slots="Body,Back,Face"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Fungal,Protoplasmic"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:vat,node:graft"
             Cost="30" Bits="002" StaffDays="8"
             Preserved="1"
             Creeds="-Templar">
    <discloses Text="your body puffs spore-gas at anything adjacent that is not your ally, on its own judgment and not yours" />
    <discloses Text="a neutral in your own market counts as not-your-ally, and the gas does not spare your city" />
    <discloses Text="you are the one thing it will never infect" />
    <discloses Text="hostile things stop wanting to stand next to you" />
    <discloses Text="fungal infections stop taking on you at all" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    ReflectDamage registers no string events at all (D/XRL/World/Parts/ReflectDamage.cs:22-29): it
    answers BeforeApplyDamageEvent, which fires on the thing being damaged, so it must sit on the
    body. IActivePart with no ChargeUse and WorksOnSelf set in the constructor (:15-20), so it runs
    on unpowered flesh, and it is loop-guarded by the "reflected" damage attribute (:34).

    THE FIRST OF TWO. See mirrorcarapace below: the class ships split, told apart by a band on
    ReflectPercentage (a public int, :15), because 5 and 100 are not the same product.
  -->
  <procedure Key="quartzhide" DisplayName="the quartz hide"
             Class="II" Grants="ReflectDamage"
             Slots="Body,Back"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Crystal,Chitin,Bone"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             Magnitude="ReflectPercentage:1-25"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="30" Bits="002" StaffDays="8"
             Preserved="1">
    <discloses Text="a little of what is done to you happens to whoever did it" />
    <discloses Text="how little is exactly what it was on the thing you took it from" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    THE SECOND OF TWO, and the reason the band exists. A hide taken from something that gives back
    everything is permanent full thorns, and at the hall's price that is degenerate. So it is
    theatre work, priced like theatre work, and the founder who wants it goes and finds the thing
    that has it. QUESTION-BACKLOG QB-10, provisional (b).
  -->
  <procedure Key="mirrorcarapace" DisplayName="the mirror carapace"
             Class="II" Grants="ReflectDamage"
             Slots="Body,Back"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Crystal,Chitin,Bone"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             Magnitude="ReflectPercentage:26-100"
             MinRung="3" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="90" Bits="0034" StaffDays="16"
             Preserved="3">
    <discloses Text="what is done to you happens to whoever did it, in something close to full measure" />
    <discloses Text="how close is exactly what it was on the thing you took it from" />
    <discloses Text="it does not choose its moments and it does not know who your friends are" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    GiantHands registers no string events (D/XRL/World/Parts/GiantHands.cs:17-24): it answers
    GetSlotsRequiredEvent, which is asked of the wearer, so body-attach. WorksOnSelf, no charge.

    Its one lawful source is a single rare heroic creature. One creature, one graft, a hunt - which
    reads exactly like a named procedure and is honestly ordinary, so it belongs here in the
    catalogue and not on that shelf. Rung 3 and the scarcity price it between them.

    Implementation note the record carries because the grant verb has to: the part's own re-equip
    housekeeping (:38-48) only runs on implant events, so a part-grant takes effect at the next
    equip. KingdomProcedures calls WantToReequip after the graft, exactly as
    CyberneticsGraftedMirrorArm does (D/.../CyberneticsGraftedMirrorArm.cs:32).
  -->
  <procedure Key="trollkingsgrip" DisplayName="the troll-king's grip"
             Class="II" Grants="GiantHands"
             Slots="Hands,Hand"
             SlotCategories="Animal"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="3" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="60" Bits="0023" StaffDays="12"
             Preserved="2">
    <discloses Text="everything you hold takes one hand less to hold" />
    <discloses Text="a thing built for two hands can be swung in one" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    Swarmer answers AttackerRollMeleeToHitEvent on the bearer and therefore belongs on the body.
    Its value is explicitly social: alone it grants nothing; beside other hostile-to-the-target
    bearers it adds the source instance's own hit/penetration bonus. Ordinary pack creatures and
    alpha variants carry different ExtraBonus fields, which DeepCopy preserves without a new
    ethnicity or creature-name table. Reopened from QB-12 for the living-cohort v1.
  -->
  <procedure Key="packstooth" DisplayName="the pack's tooth"
             Class="II" Grants="Swarmer"
             Slots="Head,Face,Body"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="20" Bits="002" StaffDays="5"
             Preserved="1">
    <discloses Text="alone, it does nothing at all" />
    <discloses Text="beside others who bear the pack's instinct and oppose your target, your natural attacks strike truer and deeper" />
    <discloses Text="the measure is exactly what lived in the creature you brought home" />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    TemperatureVenting answers bearer turn and temperature events, so body-attach is exact. Its
    sole vanilla source is nephal ground and the rite gate remains mandatory. The emitted gas is
    ownerless and may harm companions: that cost is the feature's Qud-shaped friction, disclosed
    before commitment. Reopened from QB-11; Cold Regard remains a distinct once-ever named chord.
  -->
  <procedure Key="nephalsfurnace" DisplayName="the nephal's furnace"
             Class="II" Grants="TemperatureVenting"
             Slots="Body,Back"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Crystal,Bone"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="3" Knowledge="rite:Girsh,node:graft"
             Cost="70" Bits="0034" StaffDays="14"
             Preserved="3">
    <discloses Text="after heat or cold holds you past its threshold, your body vents toward temperate blood" />
    <discloses Text="the steam or cryogenic gas has no loyalty and may burn or freeze whoever stands beside you" />
    <discloses Text="the hall asks proof of the Girsh rite because no lesser source carries this furnace" />
  </procedure>

  <!-- ================================================================== CLASS IV ==
    THE FOUR NAMED PROCEDURES. Once, ever, per founder. Each is tied to one thing the world
    actually contains; each is DISCOVERED out there and is never listed here, on any menu, or in
    any refusal until it has been found (Addendum 14 at full strength, Addendum 20's hidden clause).

    They are data-shaped like everything else so that a modder can read them and a later wave can
    re-price them, and they are gated by the accessor rather than by discipline: the slate asks
    whether the founder has heard of one before it draws a row, so "cannot have it" and "have never
    heard of it" are the same absence of a row rather than two renderings.
  -->

  <!--
    You weep water. In a mod whose whole spine is water-as-covenant this is the most loaded thing
    the lab can do to a person, and it wants a creature found, killed and carried home - a thing
    that can be lost on the road like anything else.
  -->
  <procedure Key="weepinggraft" DisplayName="the Weeping Graft"
             Class="IV" Grants="LiquidFont"
             Slots="Back,Fungal Outcrop,Body"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Fungal,Plant,Protoplasmic"
             Source="mutation" Attach="body"
             MinRung="3" Knowledge="node:graft,node:vat"
             Cost="150" Bits="0034" StaffDays="20"
             Preserved="4">
    <discloses Text="you weep water, and you go on weeping it" />
    <discloses Text="it is grafted at the lowest measure the hall can hold it at, and the hall cannot be talked higher" />
    <discloses Text="once, ever. There is no second one of these for you." />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    THE ONE DIE THIS SYSTEM ROLLS. Everything else in this file is what the slate said, because a
    thing that cost a season and a fortune may not roll dice. This one is confessedly a gamble and
    is priced as one, and it says so above the founder's hand before the hand moves.

    Drawn through the settlement kernel, so the same confession on the same save is the same limb
    after a reload: a gamble the founder takes once, not one the save file re-takes every time it
    is opened.

    Refused to a founder who already has Chimera - they get this for free and always did.
  -->
  <procedure Key="chimericconfession" DisplayName="the Chimeric Confession"
             Class="IV" Grants="AddChimericBodyPart"
             Slots="Body"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Protoplasmic,Mollusk,Jelly,Fungal,Plant"
             Source="limb" Attach="body"
             MinRung="3" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="120" Bits="0034" StaffDays="18"
             Preserved="4">
    <discloses Text="you get a limb. The hall does not know which one, and neither will you until it is done." />
    <discloses Text="this is the only thing the hall will ever do to you that it cannot tell you the outcome of" />
    <discloses Text="once, ever. There is no second one of these for you." />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    One chord, through the game's own blueprint-to-chord registry. Gated on the rite, and the rite's
    own water pays in the liquid this procedure needs as its catalyst - which is not a coincidence
    and is not machinery of ours.

    Cold Regard remains the only once-ever named nephal product. QB-11's ordinary venting product
    was later reopened for v1, keeps the same rite gate, and does not replace this chord.
  -->
  <procedure Key="coldregard" DisplayName="the Cold Regard"
             Class="IV" Grants="NephalChord"
             Slots="Face,Head"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="3" Knowledge="rite:Girsh,node:graft"
             Cost="180" Bits="00345" StaffDays="24"
             Preserved="4">
    <discloses Text="you carry one of their chords, and things that know what that is will treat you accordingly" />
    <discloses Text="it wants their own water, poured the way they pour it" />
    <discloses Text="once, ever. There is no second one of these for you." />
  </procedure>

  <!--
    A light source grown out of you. The vanilla precedent adds exactly this part type
    (D/XRL/World/Parts/LuminousInfection.cs:47), so the anatomy change is the game's own and not
    ours. The cheapest of the four and the only one the hall can do rather than the theatre, because
    it is the only one that does not change what you are - it changes what you are carrying, and it
    happens to be carrying it in a rib.
  -->
  <procedure Key="lanternrib" DisplayName="the Lantern Rib"
             Class="IV" Grants="ActiveLightSource"
             Slots="Body,Back,Icy Outcrop"
             SlotCategories="Animal,Arthropod,Fungal,Crystal,Bone"
             Source="part" Attach="body"
             MinRung="2" Knowledge="node:graft"
             Cost="70" Bits="0023" StaffDays="10"
             Preserved="2">
    <discloses Text="you carry a light that is part of you, and it can be shut" />
    <discloses Text="a light nobody can take off you is a light you cannot hide behind" />
    <discloses Text="once, ever. There is no second one of these for you." />
  </procedure>

</kingdomprocedures>
